  "notes": "Classical Sanskrit (Pāṇinian standard, c. 4th c. BCE onward). Five-point place-of-articulation system: velar, palatal, retroflex, dental, labial. Four-way laryngeal contrast (plain/aspirated × voiced/voiceless). No short /e/ or /o/ (only long). ⟨व⟩ = [ʋ] labiodental approximant. Sandhi rules are extensive but modeled at the allophone level here. Short ⟨अ⟩ (inherent vowel) is [ɐ], not a genuine mid-central schwa [ə]: Whitney's Sanskrit Grammar (1889) §21 describes it as the \"neutral vowel\", equating it to \"English so-called short u, of but, son, blood\" — a near-open central/back vowel, not the higher, laxer schwa of unstressed English syllables. Long ⟨आ⟩ is correspondingly [ɑː]: Whitney §19 places it \"in the 'Continental' or 'Italian' manner — as in far or farther\", i.e. a fully back open vowel, distinct in quality from short a, not merely longer. ⟨र⟩ is not a trill: Whitney §52 states it \"resembles the English smooth r, and, like this, seems to have been untrilled\", and §52a that \"no authority hints at a vibration as belonging to it\" — this only rules out the trill [r] this spec previously shipped, since Whitney's nineteenth-century articulatory terms do not distinguish a tap from an approximant. The specific value [ɾ] (alveolar tap) rests instead on a measured argument, not this citation: in san_deva_broad.tsv, words containing ⟨र⟩ realise it as ɾ 6404 times, plain r 133 times, ɽ twice and never as an approximant. The conjunct ⟨त्र⟩ carries the same tap. The syllabic ⟨ऋ ॠ ऌ⟩ series is left as r̩/r̩ː/l̩ — the gold uses that same convention (r̩ 80, ɾ 3 in words containing ⟨ऋ⟩), which is the standard IPA notation for a syllabic rhotic and does not itself assert a trill vs. tap articulation the way the plain consonant symbol does.",
